    "result_text": "All tests pass. The `coreStoresValidPostcode` test asserting `$.postcode == \"2000\"` confirms the regenerated DTOs carry the field end-to-end, and `errorRejectsMalformedPostcode` confirms the 400 path works on POST (where no `@Valid` runs).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional, validated `postcode` for owners, keeping each concern in a small dedicated unit:\n\n**Range lookup (new util)** \u2014 `util/PostcodeRange.java`: the pinned region\u2192range table (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099) with `isValidForCity(city, postcode)`. It reuses the existing `CityRegion.localityOf(...)` to map city\u2192region, so an unknown region falls through to \"accept any\". No table duplication.\n\n**Validation step (new function)** \u2014 `function/owner/ValidateOwnerPostcode.java`: mirrors the existing `NormalizeOwnerEmail` pattern \u2014 skips when absent (backward-compatible), else enforces the 4-digit format and delegates the range check to `PostcodeRange`, throwing a checked `InvalidPostcodeException` (\u2192 400) otherwise.\n\n**Error handling (new escalation)** \u2014 `InvalidPostcodeException` + `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` + its escalation YAML, following the `InvalidEmailException` trio exactly (responds `400 {\"error\": ...}`).\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 inserted the `validatePostcode` step into both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml`, after email normalization and before any load/build/save, so an invalid postcode is rejected before work happens.\n\n**Persistence & contract**:\n- Added `postcode` to the OpenAPI `OwnerFields` schema (regenerates `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` with the field + a `^[0-9]{4}$` pattern for docs and the PUT `@Valid` path), so it is stored and returned.\n- Added the `postcode` field + accessors to the `Owner` entity; MapStruct maps it by name automatically (`BuildOwner`), and `ApplyOwner` (PUT) now copies it.\n- Added the `postcode` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\nThe JDBC repository was intentionally left untouched \u2014 it already omits the other newer columns (email, registration_date, etc.); tests run on the spring-data-jpa arm, so this stays consistent with the existing codebase.",
